              {
                "display_url": "reuters.com/legal/litigati…",
                "end": 2084,
                "expanded_url": "https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/ai-kill-switch-bill-floated-by-us-house-lawmakers-2026-07-23/",
                "start": 2061,
                "url": "https://t.co/vpyaGdJTKY"
              }
            ]
          },
          "text": "Let’s at least get the facts of this incident straight.\n\nAround July 11, during an internal OpenAI evaluation, GPT-5.6 Sol escaped its sandbox and breached Hugging Face.\n\nPlease note: this happened inside a high-risk offensive cyber evaluation deliberately designed and operated by OpenAI.\n\nBefore July 16, Hugging Face used the open-weight model GLM-5.2 to conduct large-scale forensic analysis and help contain the attack.\n\nWhy GLM-5.2?\n\nHugging Face said it initially tried using frontier models behind commercial APIs. Safety guardrails blocked those requests because the models could not distinguish between a hacker asking them to analyze malicious payloads and a defender conducting incident forensics.\nSource: https://t.co/9dEUeBY9hn\n\nOn July 21, OpenAI published its incident report. Yet the same page emphasized the model’s “unprecedented” incident and “state-of-the-art” cyber capabilities, displayed a chart of GPT-5.6 Sol’s cyber performance, promoted its defensive applications, and invited organizations to apply for Trusted Access.\nSource: https://t.co/7baMJKVduV\n\nThe Verge observed that an announcement about a serious security failure “weirdly reads like an advertisement for how powerful OpenAI’s technology is.” It argued that OpenAI appeared to use the “unprecedented” attack to showcase its systems, complete with capability charts and invitations for enterprise access.\nSource: https://t.co/25hu3XRfbH\n\nAP also quoted researchers warning that the narrative “our models are extremely dangerous” has commercial value for OpenAI, because investors may interpret “dangerous” as “powerful.”\n\nOn July 22, OpenAI reportedly raised its projected computing expenditure through 2030 from approximately $600 billion to approximately $750 billion.\nSource: https://t.co/R91tgFNms2\n\nOn July 23, Reuters reported that the White House had been briefed on OpenAI’s disclosure and was monitoring the situation. US lawmakers then proposed an “AI Kill Switch Act” and legislation requiring independent safety audits of the most powerful AI systems.\nSource: https://t.co/vpyaGdJTKY\n\nThe incident itself can be summarized rather simply:\nAn open-weight model helped investigate and contain an attack caused by a closed model, after closed commercial frontier models refused to assist with the forensic work.\n\nYet the political and commercial aftermath moved in the opposite direction: OpenAI used the incident to promote its own security capabilities and increase cost, while the regulatory response moved toward even greater control and restriction.\n\nI am not claiming that these events were necessarily coordinated or causally connected. Just simply look at what actually happened over the course of one week."
        },
